WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.270
<v Sean Cole>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Sean Cole. Ira Glass is going to be back next week.
<v Sean Cole>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是肖恩·科尔。艾拉·格拉斯下周会回来。

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:28.660
<v Sean Cole>So Ted Cruz dropped out of the race this week for president, and a lot of Republican voters were thrilled. But a lot of them were heartbroken and confused, asking themselves a lot of questions about themselves and their party and what they stand for. And I wanted to talk with my colleague Zoe Chace because she'd been doing a lot of campaign coverage for the show, talking to Cruz supporters.
<v Sean Cole>泰德·克鲁兹本周退出了总统竞选，许多共和党选民感到兴奋，但也有很多人感到心碎和困惑，他们开始审视自己、党派以及他们的信仰。我想和我的同事佐伊·蔡斯聊聊，因为她一直在为节目进行竞选报道，采访了许多克鲁兹的支持者。

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.460
<v Zoe Chace>Hi, Sean.
<v Zoe Chace>你好，肖恩。

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.220
<v Sean Cole>Hello.
<v Sean Cole>你好。

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:48.820
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, I was worried about the Cruz supporters, to be honest. Some people had been waiting for 30 years for somebody like Ted Cruz to get as far as he did-- this strong, Christian, very conservative evangelical candidate. And there was one guy I knew that might be pretty heartbroken, and I wanted to check in with him. I had to call Tony.
<v Zoe Chace>说实话，我当时很担心克鲁兹的支持者。有些人等了30年才等到像泰德·克鲁兹这样的人走得这么远——一位坚定的、基督教背景的、非常保守的福音派候选人。我认识一个人，知道他一定会非常难过，我想去看看他。我得给托尼打个电话。

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.090
<v Sean Cole>And just say who Tony is again.
<v Sean Cole>再介绍一下托尼是谁。

00:00:50.090 --> 00:01:06.360
<v Zoe Chace>Tony Beam is a radio host in South Carolina. Conservative, evangelical Christian talk radio. Remember, we did a story on Tony a couple months ago. And the issue was that when his evangelical listeners started embracing Donald Trump, Tony felt betrayed and confused and upset.
<v Zoe Chace>托尼·比姆是南卡罗来纳州的一名电台主持人，主持保守派福音派基督教谈话节目。还记得我们几个月前做过关于托尼的报道吗？当时的问题是，当他的福音派听众开始支持唐纳德·特朗普时，托尼感到被背叛、困惑和苦恼。

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.220
<v Sean Cole>So how is he taking the news?
<v Sean Cole>那他现在对这个消息有什么反应？

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.790
<v Zoe Chace>He couldn't even bring himself to watch it on TV.
<v Zoe Chace>他甚至无法让自己在电视上看这个消息。

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:35.990
<v Tony Beam>My wife told me. This is exactly what happened. I was sitting in the living room, playing the guitar, when my wife got home last night. And she put-- because I hadn't turned any of this stuff on. I knew what it was going to be. I couldn't take the Trump edition of Fox News. So I'm sitting there playing the guitar, and she came in and put her hand on my shoulder and said, "I'm really sorry. I just heard that Cruz dropped out."
<v Tony Beam>是我妻子告诉我的。事情就是这样。昨晚我妻子回家时，我正坐在客厅弹吉他。她走进来——因为我一直没打开电视，我知道会看到什么，我实在无法忍受福克斯新闻的“特朗普版”。我正坐着弹吉他，她走过来把手搭在我的肩膀上说：“真的很抱歉，我刚听说克鲁兹退出了。”

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.600
<v Zoe Chace>What song were you playing?
<v Zoe Chace>你在弹什么歌？

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:51.850
<v Tony Beam>I'm just learning. Believe it or not, I'm 58 years old, and I'm trying to learn to play the guitar. So I was playing a little Creedence Clearwater Revival because I'm a big John Fogerty fan. So I was playing "Have You Ever Seen the Rain?"
<v Tony Beam>刚在学。信不信由你，我58岁了，正在试着学弹吉他。当时我在弹一点清水乐团的歌，因为我是约翰·福格蒂的铁杆粉丝。我在弹《你见过雨吗？》。

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.560
<v Tony Beam>[MUSIC - "HAVE YOU EVER SEEN THE RAIN?"]
<v Tony Beam>[音乐 - 《你见过雨吗？》]

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:07.490
<v Sean Cole>It occurs to me, Zoe, that a lot of people who were upset about this, they can sort of just go hide or drink somewhere. And he has to be on the radio, upset.
<v Sean Cole>佐伊，我想到一点，很多对此感到难过的人，他们可以躲起来或者去喝一杯。但他必须要在电台上表现出难过。

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:11.920
<v Zoe Chace>He has to go on the radio, live, at 7:00 in the morning with all his feelings.
<v Zoe Chace>他得在早上7点直播，带着所有的情绪面对听众。

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.330
<v Tony Beam>"All right, good morning, everybody. The world keeps turning."
<v Tony Beam>“好了，大家早上好。世界仍在转动。”

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:27.170
<v Tony Beam>I cracked the microphone and I said, OK, it's a Trump kind of day. The Trumpists are trumpeting. And so here's my question to you listening to the show.
<v Tony Beam>我打开麦克风说，好吧，今天是“特朗普日”。特朗普的支持者们正在大声疾呼。那么，我想问问正在听节目的各位一个问题。

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:32.170
<v Tony Beam>"The question of the day is, are you going to vote for Hillary or one of her top donors?"
<v Tony Beam>“今天的问题是，你是打算投票给希拉里，还是投票给她的主要捐赠者之一？”

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.560
<v Zoe Chace>He's talking about Donald Trump.
<v Zoe Chace>他在说唐纳德·特朗普。

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:43.420
<v Tony Beam>"That's pretty much the choice that we're left with. We've got Hillary or a Hillary supporter. So there you go."
<v Tony Beam>“这基本上就是我们剩下的选择了。我们要么选希拉里，要么选希拉里的支持者。情况就是这样。”

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:49.350
<v Zoe Chace>Tony is never going to get behind Donald Trump. He sees him as a fake conservative. He's just an opportunist.
<v Zoe Chace>托尼永远不会支持唐纳德·特朗普。在他眼里，特朗普是个虚伪的保守派，只是个投机分子。

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:50.570
<v Sean Cole>He doesn't share his values.
<v Sean Cole>他与托尼的价值观不同。

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.750
<v Zoe Chace>Right, he just grabs principles when he wants them and then just drops them when he doesn't anymore.
<v Zoe Chace>没错，他只是在需要的时候利用原则，不需要的时候就抛弃它们。

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.470
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:15.900
<v Zoe Chace>Tony has built his entire life around Christian values, and he doesn't think that Trump has those. And now he's in this situation where he's looking ahead over the next couple months. He has a live radio show, two hours every single day. He's going to be dealing with this. So I asked him, Tony, what is the plan? How are you going to handle this on the radio?
<v Zoe Chace>托尼的一生都建立在基督教价值观之上，他不认为特朗普拥有这些。现在他陷入了这种境地，他还要展望未来几个月。他每天有两小时的现场广播节目，他不得不面对这一切。所以我问他：托尼，有什么计划？你在电台上打算怎么处理这件事？

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:29.050
<v Tony Beam>I'm just going to talk about what's going on. It's going to be kind of like there's been a wreck. And I'm going to describe it. You know, the rescue efforts.
<v Tony Beam>我就谈论正在发生的事情。这就像是发生了车祸。我会描述现场，你知道，描述救援工作。

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.490
<v Zoe Chace>Like you're reporting on a big natural disaster or something.
<v Zoe Chace>就像你在报道一场大型自然灾害一样。

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.850
<v Tony Beam>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Tony Beam>是的，是的。

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:39.370
<v Zoe Chace>What do you think your listeners want you to do? What are they looking for from you?
<v Zoe Chace>你觉得你的听众希望你怎么做？他们想从你这里得到什么？

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:55.620
<v Tony Beam>Unity. Hold hands. Sing "Kumbaya." And to be fair, listen, I've been much more conservative than our nominee for the last three or four cycles.
<v Tony Beam>团结一致。携手同行，共唱《团结一致》。说实话，在过去三四届选举中，我一直比我们的候选人更保守。

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.130
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, Mitt Romney, John McCain.
<v Zoe Chace>对，米特·罗姆尼，约翰·麦凯恩。

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:11.760
<v Tony Beam>Yeah, Romney, John McCain, Bob Dole. So I've always told people, look, the primary is where we make a decision as a party. And then we've got to come together and vote for the nominee.
<v Tony Beam>是的，罗姆尼、麦凯恩、鲍勃·多尔。所以我总是告诉人们，看，初选是我们作为一个党派做出决定的过程，然后我们必须团结起来，投票给胜出的候选人。

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:20.710
<v Tony Beam>And so now I've got people pounding on me. Well, you're a big hypocrite because your man lost, and now you won't unify. You won't get behind Trump.
<v Tony Beam>所以现在有人在攻击我，说我是一个大伪君子，因为我支持的人输了，现在我不肯团结了。我不肯支持特朗普。

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:36.960
<v Tony Beam>Well, to me it's a completely different scenario. I've never felt like I was voting for the lesser of two evils. I didn't think those guys were evil. I thought they were different from me. I wished they were more conservative.
<v Tony Beam>但在我看来，这是完全不同的情况。我从没觉得自己在“两害相权取其轻”。我不觉得那些人是“坏”的。我认为他们只是和我不同。我希望他们更保守一些。

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.840
<v Zoe Chace>Right, they just weren't your top pick.
<v Zoe Chace>对，他们只是不是你的首选。

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:49.660
<v Tony Beam>That wasn't my top pick. I mean, I can't vote for Donald Trump for president.
<v Tony Beam>那不是我的首选。我是说，我无法投票给唐纳德·特朗普当总统。

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.690
<v Sean Cole>So when you did the first story, there was this guy.
<v Sean Cole>当你做第一个报道时，有个家伙。

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:53.380
<v Zoe Chace>Barry.
<v Zoe Chace>巴里。

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.130
<v Sean Cole>Yes, Barry, who was like the Trump interpreter.
<v Sean Cole>是的，巴里，他就像是特朗普的翻译官。

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:59.360
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, he called Tony all the time to harass him about Donald Trump.
<v Zoe Chace>对，他总是打电话给托尼，骚扰他关于唐纳德·特朗普的事。

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.700
<v Sean Cole>Yes. And did he call this time?
<v Sean Cole>没错。那他这次有打电话吗？

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:10.670
<v Zoe Chace>Of course. He called the morning after Cruz had dropped out. Tony said, of course Barry called him and tried to sell him on Trump and party unity.
<v Zoe Chace>当然。克鲁兹退出后的第二天早上他就打了。托尼说，巴里当然找过他，试图说服他支持特朗普和党内团结。

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:28.190
<v Tony Beam>Barry and I, look, we have a good relationship. And he was telling me-- he said, "man, there's room for you, brother. You can get on the Trump train with me." He said, it's not-- because I played the sound bite where Trump was talking about unity and we're going to love everybody.
<v Tony Beam>我和巴里，听着，我们关系不错。他当时对我说：“伙计，兄弟，这里有你的位子。你可以跟我一起上特朗普号列车。”他说，这不是——因为我播放了特朗普谈论团结、我们要爱每一个人的音频。

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:41.610
<v Donald Trump>"This country, which is very, very divided in so many different ways, is going to become one beautiful, loving country. And we're going to love each other. We're going to cherish each other. We're going to take care of each other. And we're going to have--"
<v Donald Trump>“这个在许多方面都非常分裂的国家，将会变成一个美丽、充满爱的国家。我们要相亲相爱，要珍惜彼此，要互相关照。我们将会拥有——”

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:47.260
<v Tony Beam>And then Barry said, "yeah, man, this is not the Trump Train. This is the Love Train. You've got to get on board."
<v Tony Beam>然后巴里说：“是啊，伙计，这已经不是‘特朗普列车’了。这是‘爱之列车’。你得加入我们。”

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:50.280
<v Barry Chisholm>"It's not the Trump Train anymore. It's the Love Train."
<v Barry Chisholm>“这不再是特朗普列车了。这是爱之列车。”

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.950
<v Tony Beam>"It's the what? Love Train?"
<v Tony Beam>“这是什么？爱之列车？”

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:53.800
<v Tony Beam>[LAUGHTER]
<v Tony Beam>[笑声]

00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:16.660
<v Tony Beam>"Bob, you've got to find-- I've got Bob over here. Bob's got to find "The Love Train" by The O'Jays. So we'll pull that up in just a minute. We're going to have another Woodstock. I mean, it's going to be 1969 all over where there will be people singing, are you going to San Francisco? It's going to be a Republican love-in. Not."
<v Tony Beam>“鲍勃，你得找到——我这边有鲍勃。鲍勃得找到O'Jays合唱团的《爱之列车》。我们一会儿就放这首歌。我们要举办另一场伍德斯托克音乐节。我是说，这会重现1969年，人们会唱歌，‘你要去旧金山吗？’这会是一场共和党的爱的大联欢。才怪。”

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:41.930
<v Zoe Chace>Tony remembers those first Republican debates when Trump was talking and saying all this outlandish stuff. And his listeners were calling in and being like, we love this guy, and this guy is speaking for me. And it just felt so viscerally wrong to him, like he was like, this is not who we are. This is not who we Republicans are. And then he wakes up one morning, and Cruz is out.
<v Zoe Chace>托尼还记得最初的共和党辩论，当时特朗普在说那些离谱的话。他的听众打电话进来，说他们喜欢这个家伙，他代表了他们的心声。这让托尼感到本质上的不对劲，他觉得：这不是我们。这不是我们共和党人。然后一天早上醒来，克鲁兹出局了。

00:06:41.930 --> 00:07:19.170
<v Tony Beam>Today I have to face the fact that maybe that is who we are. I mean, you realize 6-- what was it? You've probably seen the numbers. 6 or 7 out of 10 Republicans-- I think it's been consistently 6 out of 10 agree that we ought to ban all Muslims from coming to the United States. And so maybe this whole thing is a collective voice saying to me, you think this is not who we are? Yeah, it is. Wake up, dude. This is--
<v Tony Beam>今天我不得不面对这样一个事实：也许这确实就是我们。我的意思是，你意识到6……是多少来着？你大概看过数据。十分之六或七的共和党人——我想一直以来都有十分之六的人同意我们应该禁止所有穆斯林进入美国。所以，也许这一切是一个集体的声音在对我说：你觉得这不是我们？是的，这就是我们。醒醒吧，伙计。这就是——

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:54.360
<v Tony Beam>I have an atheist that calls me pretty regularly. And he gives me what for on the issues, particularly moral issues. But we've had some pretty good give-and-takes too, philosophically. But one of the questions he always puts to me is, how does it feel to be on the wrong side of history? And my answer has always been, a lot better than it would feel to be on the wrong side of eternity. My values are deeper than the culture of the moment.
<v Tony Beam>有个无神论者经常给我打电话。他会在议题上，特别是在道德议题上质问我。但在哲学层面，我们也进行过一些不错的交流。他总是问我一个问题是：站在历史错误的一边是什么感觉？而我的回答总是：这比站在永恒错误的一边要好得多。我的价值观比当下的文化更深刻。

00:07:54.360 --> 00:08:09.810
<v Tony Beam>And I don't mean that in a-- golly, that sounded-- I don't mean that in an arrogant way. I mean it in a broken way that says, I realize that I may be swimming against the tide.
<v Tony Beam>我不是以一种——天哪，那听起来——我不是以傲慢的方式说这句话。我是以一种心碎的方式说的，意思是，我意识到我可能是在逆流而上。

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.000
<v Zoe Chace>So are you a Republican?
<v Zoe Chace>那你现在还是共和党人吗？

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:38.420
<v Tony Beam>Yeah, I'm still a Republican today because I haven't done anything about it. But it would break my heart to not be associated anymore. I mean, some people can just walk away. I mean, it would break my heart. But I'm not going to stay in the Republican Party if the Republican Party is not going to be conservative and it's not going to stand for traditional values.
<v Tony Beam>是的，我今天还是共和党人，因为我还没做出什么改变。但如果不再和这个党派有联系，我会心碎的。我是说，有些人可以转头就走。对我来说这会心碎。但如果共和党不再保守，不再捍卫传统价值观，我就不会留在共和党内。

00:08:38.420 --> 00:09:12.040
<v Sean Cole>It's jarring when you're rolling along with a group of people, for years sometimes. They're like family. And then you hit this one dividing point that makes you think, wait, is that who we are? Because if that's who we are, what does that say about who I am? Even if the "we" is just you and one other person, it's hard. And that's what our show is about today, in two acts-- two families with two very different contentions. Stay with us.
<v Sean Cole>当你和一群人一起前行时，有时一走就是几年。他们就像家人一样。然后你遇到了一个分歧点，让你想：等等，这就是我们吗？因为如果这就是我们，那说明我是什么样的人？即使这个“我们”只有你和另一个人，也很难。而这正是今天我们节目的主题，分两幕展开——两个家庭，有着两种完全不同的争论。请继续收听。

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:39.080
<v Sean Cole>Act One, "Whose Great Idea was This?" This first story deals with something controversial that's covered now and then in the news. But this is the first time I've heard it talked about so personally as opposed to abstract and far away. It comes to us from Mariya Karimjee, and I'll let her explain what's at issue. But I just want to say here that, as Ira sometimes says, this story does acknowledge the existence of sex, and it probably isn't right for young kids. OK, here's Mariya.
<v Sean Cole>第一幕，“这是谁的好主意？”第一个故事涉及现在新闻中时有报道的一个争议性话题。但这是我第一次听到有人如此私人地谈论它，而不是以一种抽象、遥远的方式。故事来自玛丽亚·卡里姆吉，我会让她来解释问题所在。但我在这里想先说明一点，正如艾拉有时所说，这个故事涉及性的存在，可能不适合年幼的孩子。好了，请听玛丽亚的讲述。

00:09:39.080 --> 00:10:01.980
<v Mariya Karimjee>When I was in high school, my mother caught me reading a grocery store Harlequin novel in my room. She raised one eyebrow as I shoved it under the covers. It wasn't until weeks later, driving me somewhere, that she mentioned she'd seen it. I remember the firm line of her mouth as she explained that sex was almost never a good feeling. Don't go looking for that, she told me. That's almost never true.
<v Mariya Karimjee>高中时，我妈发现我在房间里看哈勒昆超市言情小说。当我把它塞到被子里时，她挑了挑眉。几周后，她开车送我的时候才提起这件事。我记得她解释说性几乎从来不是一种好的感受时，那坚定的嘴唇线条。别去寻找那个，她告诉我。那几乎从来不是真的。

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:26.460
<v Mariya Karimjee>I hadn't had sex, but I had started to wonder about it. My mother said that she thought, for men, it was the way that they described it in the books. For us, she said, it's different. At the time, I assumed that by "us" she meant women in general. Later I wondered if she meant her and me specifically.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我还没经历过性，但我开始好奇了。我妈说她觉得，对于男人来说，那是书里描述的那样。对我们来说，她说，情况不同。当时我以为她说的“我们”是指女性整体。后来我怀疑她是指她和我本人。

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:57.970
<v Mariya Karimjee>When I was seven years old and living in Karachi, Pakistan, my mother took me to the pediatrician. While I sat on a stool, polishing imaginary dirt off the buckles on my Mary Janes, my mother quietly asked if it was time for me to get the bug removed. According to my mother, a bug was growing in an egg down there-- her language, not mine-- and that it would hatch and eventually crawl to my brain unless we removed it, she said. My pediatrician agreed. It was time to see the woman who removes the bug.
