00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:57.240
<v Mariya Karimjee>Then I called my mother. "Is sex ever good for you?" I asked her. There was a brief but important pause. I heard the click of a door and then her voice, clear, unmuffled. "Excuse me?" she asked. "I tried to have sex with my boyfriend," I said. "It didn't go well. It hurt so bad that I'm not sure I ever want to try it again. I know you don't think I should be having sex, but I'm so scared."
<v Mariya Karimjee>然后我给妈妈打了电话。“你觉得性生活好吗？”我问她。一阵短暂但重要的停顿。我听到了门关上的咔哒声，接着是她的声音，清晰，不含糊。“你说什么？”她问。“我试着和男友发生关系，”我说。“过程很糟糕。太疼了，我不知道以后还想不想尝试。我知道你不认为我该发生关系，但我真的很害怕。”

00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:14.560
<v Mariya Karimjee>What I didn't say out loud, but what my mother instinctively knew, was what I was so scared of. It wasn't just that I was scared of sex but that because of that, true love would escape me forever, that I was unlovable.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我没有大声说出来，但妈妈本能地知道我在害怕什么。不仅是因为我害怕性，而且是因为正因如此，真爱将永远离我而去，我是不可爱的。

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:48.230
<v Mariya Karimjee>She told me about how when she was a teenager, she saved up pocket change for Harlequin romances. All I wanted was to figure out how to feel like those women, she told me. She also told me how when she was with my dad, if he moved too fast, she'd feel sharp pain. I knew exactly what she was talking about, pain that suffocated any of the good. "I get panic attacks when that happens," I told her. "I did too," she told me. "Your father and I talked through what felt good, and we figured out a way," she explained.
<v Mariya Karimjee>她告诉我她青少年时期如何积攒零花钱买哈勒昆浪漫小说。她告诉我，她只想弄清楚如何感受到书里那些女人的感觉。她还告诉我，她和爸爸在一起时，如果他动作太快，她会感到剧痛。我完全明白她在说什么，那是压倒一切愉悦的疼痛。“当那种情况发生时，我会惊恐发作，”我告诉她。“我也一样，”她对我说。“你父亲和我谈论过什么感觉好，我们找到了方法，”她解释说。

00:23:48.230 --> 00:24:18.700
<v Mariya Karimjee>It was a good conversation. I told my mother that I loved her. I kept wanting her to apologize for giving me the same pain that she herself had suffered, but she stayed quiet. As we said our goodbyes and were about to hang up, she suddenly said, unprompted, "I hate everyone else too. Those women on TV who love sex, who enjoy it, I hate them too."
<v Mariya Karimjee>那是一次不错的对话。我告诉妈妈我爱她。我一直希望她能为让我承受了她自己也遭受过的痛苦而道歉，但她保持了沉默。在挂断电话前的告别时刻，她突然不假思索地说道：“我也讨厌所有人。电视上那些热爱性、享受性的女人，我也讨厌她们。”

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:40.830
<v Mariya Karimjee>Since that first experience with a gynecologist in college, I've been to a handful of other doctors. Many of them hadn't seen anyone who'd been cut before. When I'd peppered them with questions, they seemed hesitant to respond with authority. Only once since my college ex did I hook up with anyone again-- try to hook up, that is. It too went badly.
<v Mariya Karimjee>自从大学第一次看妇科医生后，我又看了几位医生。她们中许多人从未见过被切割过的患者。当我追问她们时，她们似乎很犹豫，无法给予权威的回应。自从大学那次约会后，我只再和人发生过一次关系——我是说，尝试过。那次也进行得很糟。

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:59.760
<v Mariya Karimjee>That time prompted me to take action. I visited a doctor who specialized in victims of FGM. I was living without health insurance that year. And when I called her office explaining this, the receptionist told me to plan to spend somewhere around $2,000 for the consult. It was all of my savings.
<v Mariya Karimjee>那次经历促使我采取了行动。我拜访了一位专门研究FGM受害者的医生。那年我没有医疗保险。当我给她的诊室打电话解释情况时，接待员告诉我要为咨询费用预留大约2000美元。那是我所有的积蓄。

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:25.680
<v Mariya Karimjee>The money was worth it. This doctor, unlike so many of the gynecologists I'd seen before, didn't wince when she peered between my legs. She didn't over-apologize or pat my knees. She didn't murmur in a hushed whisper, like the medical resident at Columbia, "oh, bless your dear heart." Instead, she silently examined me. She'd heard of the religious sect that I belong to and had examined other girls like me.
<v Mariya Karimjee>钱花得值。这位医生，不像我之前看过的许多妇科医生，在窥视我的双腿之间时没有畏缩。她没有过度道歉或拍我的膝盖。她没有像哥伦比亚大学的住院医生那样低声私语：“噢，天哪，祝福你亲爱的。”相反，她静静地检查了我。她听说过我所属的宗教教派，也检查过其他像我一样的女孩。

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:43.010
<v Mariya Karimjee>She explained that because the cutting is done in the living room without proper medical equipment, for girls in my sect, the results varied. Some of the girls can easily go on to have great sex lives. But for me, the main difference was in the extensive scar tissue and the nerve damage.
<v Mariya Karimjee>她解释说，因为切割是在客厅里完成的，没有专业的医疗设备，所以我们教派女孩的情况各不相同。有些女孩可以轻松地继续拥有美满的性生活。但对我来说，主要的区别在于广泛的疤痕组织和神经损伤。

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:57.520
<v Mariya Karimjee>I'd read of a surgery where a doctor was grafting other genital tissue as part of a regenerative process. I asked her about it. She shook her head. "I wouldn't recommend this," she said. "The science was unreliable."
<v Mariya Karimjee>我读过一种手术，医生可以通过移植其他生殖器组织作为再生过程的一部分。我问她关于这个手术。她摇了摇头。“我不建议这么做，”她说。“科学上不可靠。”

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:15.640
<v Mariya Karimjee>She told me what I'd long suspected. I'd probably never have a wonderful, easy, uncomplicated sex life. Instead, sex for me would likely involve many careful conversations with my partner, a sex therapist, and a willingness to trust a human beyond what I could imagine.
<v Mariya Karimjee>她告诉我我早已怀疑的事情。我可能永远不会拥有美好、轻松、无复杂的性生活。相反，对我来说，性可能需要与伴侣、性治疗师进行多次谨慎的沟通，并且需要比我能想象的更进一步去信任人类。

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:50.040
<v Mariya Karimjee>I wasn't horribly mutilated or defective in a way that made me incapable of enjoying sex. She explained, "this is not terminal. This is not a life sentence. Find a sex therapist you trust. Learn to allow yourself to let go." I wasn't sure what she meant and didn't ask further questions. In the moments after she gave me her verdict, she mentioned a support group she held at Brigham and Women's Hospital. I nodded enthusiastically, knowing I'd never attend.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我没有被严重摧残或以某种方式导致我无法享受性快感。她解释说：“这不是绝症。这不是无期徒刑。找一个你信任的性治疗师。学会让自己放开。”我不确定她是什么意思，没再问问题。在给出结论后的片刻，她提到了她在布莱根和妇女医院举办的支持小组。我热切地点着头，心里却知道我永远不会去参加。

00:26:50.040 --> 00:27:14.370
<v Mariya Karimjee>A few years ago, I moved back to Karachi for work. My parents had moved back while I was in college. Here's a quick little explainer about Karachi, just so you know what to picture. Karachi is a huge city with around 22 million people. It's modern, and it's not. It has gleaming, gorgeous, multi-story malls and large movie theaters that play the latest Hollywood and Bollywood films. People are on Tinder.
<v Mariya Karimjee>几年前，我为了工作搬回了卡拉奇。父母在我大学期间搬了回去。这里有一个关于卡拉奇的快速小介绍，让你知道画面是什么样的。卡拉奇是一个拥有2200万人口的超级大城市。它既现代，又不现代。它有闪亮、华丽的多层购物中心和播放最新好莱坞与宝莱坞电影的大型电影院。人们也在用Tinder。

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:28.570
<v Mariya Karimjee>Karachi also gets listed as among the most dangerous mega cities in the world. There's no international tourism. There's beggars at almost every street corner and piles of burning trash pretty much everywhere in the city, even the really nice areas.
<v Mariya Karimjee>卡拉奇也被列为世界上最危险的特大城市之一。这里没有国际旅游业。每个街角几乎都有乞丐，而且几乎随处可见燃烧的垃圾堆，即使在非常好的地区也是如此。

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:41.360
<v Mariya Karimjee>The Dawoodi Bohra sect, which I'm from, is a small, typically pretty well-off minority in Karachi. There's about 40,000 of us. I live in a neighborhood made up entirely of other Bohris.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我出身的达伍迪波拉教派在卡拉奇是一个人数较少、通常较富裕的少数群体。我们大约有4万人。我住在一个完全由波拉人组成的社区。

00:27:41.360 --> 00:28:08.770
<v Mariya Karimjee>Readjusting to life in Karachi as an adult was difficult. I reluctantly attended services at the same mosque I went to as a kid. I'd sit there and scan all of the faces to see if I could recognize the woman who had cut me. But then I realized that seated beside me were at least 100 other girls who had likely also been cut. I wondered what their sex lives were like. I'd sit fidgeting, wondering if any of them had escaped the cutting.
<v Mariya Karimjee>作为成年人重新适应卡拉奇的生活很困难。我勉强参加了儿时去过的同一家清真寺的礼拜。我会坐在那里扫视所有人的脸，看看能不能认出那个给我动刀的女人。但随后我意识到，坐在我旁边的至少还有100个女孩，她们很可能也经历过同样的切割。我好奇她们的性生活是什么样的。我会烦躁不安地坐着，想知道她们中是否有人逃过了切割。

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:16.330
<v Mariya Karimjee>So I started asking women in Karachi to talk to me about this. Half of the women I asked said no. This woman said yes.
<v Mariya Karimjee>所以我开始请求卡拉奇的女性和我聊这个话题。我问过的一半女性拒绝了。这个女人答应了。

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:19.650
<v Sameena>I mean, whatever I can answer, I will.
<v Sameena>我是说，我能回答的，我都会回答。

00:28:19.650 --> 00:28:21.600
<v Mariya Karimjee>Are you nervous?
<v Mariya Karimjee>你紧张吗？

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:22.970
<v Sameena>Not so much.
<v Sameena>没那么紧张。

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:37.120
<v Mariya Karimjee>This is a friend of mine from childhood. She asked that I not use her real name, so I'm going to call her Sameena. Sameena is 27. We were friends when we were very young, but we didn't stay in touch after I moved to the US.
<v Mariya Karimjee>这是一位我儿时的朋友。她要求我不要用真名，所以我就叫她萨米娜。萨米娜27岁。我们很小的时候是朋友，但我搬到美国后我们就没联系了。

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:46.800
<v Mariya Karimjee>We sat and talked on my bedroom floor. Sameena said she'd been cut too. Unlike me, though, she knew the woman who did it. She was a distant relative.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我们坐在我卧室的地板上聊天。萨米娜说她也经历过切割。不过与我不同，她认识那个动手的人。那是她的一位远房亲戚。

00:28:46.800 --> 00:29:07.250
<v Sameena>I remember that we were in her drawing room. I was lying on the floor. I think my mom was holding me. And she was holding my legs, and my mom must have been holding my arms and stuff. There was no-- I don't remember what equipment she used. Was it a blade or something else? I don't remember. She cut something.
<v Sameena>我记得我们在她的客厅里。我躺在地板上。我想是我妈抱着我。她按住我的腿，我妈一定是在按住我的手臂之类的。没有——我不记得她用了什么器械。是刀还是别的什么？我不记得了。她割了点东西。

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:08.740
<v Mariya Karimjee>Do you remember it hurting?
<v Mariya Karimjee>你记得疼吗？

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:31.700
<v Sameena>I think so, yes, enough that I was screaming and crying. And then she put a bandage over it. And then she had this huge scissor, and I thought she was cutting something else again. She was bringing like a huge-ass scissors, so I screamed and I jumped. And then my mom held me and all of that. And then she said in Gujarati that she's cutting the bandage, not me again. And then she gave me candy.
<v Sameena>我想是的，很疼，疼到我一直在尖叫哭泣。然后她贴上了一块绷带。她还有一把巨大的剪刀，我以为她又要割什么东西了。她拿着像巨大剪刀一样的东西，所以我尖叫着跳了起来。我妈抱住了我。然后她用古吉拉特语说，她在割绷带，不是又割我。然后她给了我一颗糖。

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:47.730
<v Sameena>And then I came home. And I knew that something had been cut, but obviously I didn't know why because we never spoke about it with friends and stuff. I mean, my mom told me, don't talk about it. I was told it was a bug. It was a bug. It's gone. You don't have it anymore. Chapter closed.
<v Sameena>然后我回家了。我知道有些地方被割了，但显然我不知道为什么，因为我们从不和朋友提起这种事。我是说，我妈告诉我，别提这件事。我被告知那是只“虫子”。那是只虫子。它没了。你再也没有它了。篇章翻过去了。

00:29:47.730 --> 00:30:19.190
<v Mariya Karimjee>The bug story. I had thought that was specific to my family. Like me, Sameena figured out on her own what had been done to her. When she was 17, she read a change.org petition asking Bohris to ban clitorectomies. Almost every Bohri I know has seen it. Sameena thought it was probably drafted and circulated by Bohris who weren't as devout as she was. Why are they questioning this? she thought. Then she did the same thing I did. She asked her mother about it.
<v Mariya Karimjee>虫子的故事。我曾以为那是我们家特有的。像我一样，萨米娜自己搞清楚了在她身上发生了什么。17岁时，她读到了Change.org上的一份请愿书，呼吁波拉人禁止阴蒂切除术。我认识的几乎每个波拉人都看过它。萨米娜认为这可能是由那些不像她那么虔诚的波拉人起草并传播的。为什么他们会质疑这个？她想。然后她做了和我一样的事。她问了她妈妈。

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:45.970
<v Sameena>My intention was not to speak to her in detail and ask her why she did it or why did they have to do it. It was like [GUJARATI], the quick question, why do we do it? And then she said, to curb the sexual desire of women so that they don't cheat and they don't get horny and sleep around. And she didn't use these words, but she said that this is why we do it, to curb your sexual desire.
<v Sameena>我的意图并不是要和她详细谈论并质问她为什么这么做，或者他们为什么要这么做。那就像是一次简短的质问：为什么我们要这样做？然后她说，是为了克制女性的性欲，这样她们就不会出轨，不会变得欲火焚身，到处和人乱搞。她没用这些词，但她说这就是我们做这件事的原因，为了克制你的性欲。

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:49.050
<v Mariya Karimjee>Were you surprised by her answer?
<v Mariya Karimjee>她的回答让你惊讶吗？

00:30:49.050 --> 00:31:02.630
<v Sameena>I wasn't surprised as such. I just felt like it was unfair. Why do you want to stop me from having casual sex? Why not men? So I think that's what I thought at that point, but I still don't remember feeling angry.
<v Sameena>算不上惊讶。我只是觉得这不公平。为什么你要阻止我进行随意的性行为？为什么不限制男性？所以我想这就是我当时的想法，但我依然不记得有感到愤怒。

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:06.020
<v Mariya Karimjee>That was 10 years ago. She feels differently now.
<v Mariya Karimjee>那是10年前的事了。她现在感觉不同了。

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:16.180
<v Sameena>Right now I do feel some anger that this is done. I had a huge argument with my parents and, well, it didn't end up anywhere.
<v Sameena>现在我确实对这种习俗感到愤怒。我和父母大吵了一架，嗯，没吵出什么结果。

00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:18.520
<v Mariya Karimjee>When was this argument?
<v Mariya Karimjee>那次争论是什么时候？

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:19.720
<v Sameena>Recent.
<v Sameena>最近。

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:29.100
<v Mariya Karimjee>Recent as in February of this year, three months ago. When she says this, I know exactly what prompted the argument. It was my brother.
<v Mariya Karimjee>最近是指今年二月，三个月前。当她说这话时，我知道是什么引发了那场争论。是我弟弟。

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:50.600
<v Mariya Karimjee>In February, he spoke out against FGM, female genital mutilation, at a panel at the UN. We were asked to do the panel together, but I was out of the country, so he did it alone. He sat at a long, curved conference table in a stately room with wood walls. He was one of five people who spoke that day. It was videotaped.
<v Mariya Karimjee>二月时，他在联合国的一个小组会议上公开反对FGM，即女性生殖器残割。我们要一起参加那个小组，但我当时在国外，所以他一个人去了。他坐在一间带有木墙的庄严会议室里，在那张长长的弧形会议桌旁。他是那天发言的五个人之一。会议录了像。

00:31:50.600 --> 00:32:04.620
<v Ammar Karimjee>"So many women in my life that I was close to had gone through this. And I was just shocked. I was in denial. I was frustrated. It's been over the last five to six years since then that I've really become involved in this movement."
<v Ammar Karimjee>“我生命中许多与我亲近的女性都经历过这一切。我简直惊呆了。我一直处于否认状态。我感到非常挫败。过去五到六年来，我才真正投入到这场运动中。”

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:24.240
<v Mariya Karimjee>My brother learned about FGM in his college anthropology class. He thought it was something that happened in rural African villages. He had no idea about me or our mom and grandmothers. Then a Bohri girl at college set him straight, and he called me immediately, saying, "why didn't you tell me?"
<v Mariya Karimjee>我弟弟是在大学人类学课上了解到FGM的。他以为这只是发生在非洲乡村的小事。他根本不知道发生在我身上，以及我们妈妈和祖母身上的事。后来大学里一个波拉女孩让他看清了真相，他立刻打电话给我，说：“你为什么不告诉我？”

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:48.060
<v Mariya Karimjee>After he spoke at the UN, he posted about it on Facebook. It's a long, passionate post where he urges Bohri men not to stay silent and condone FGM. In the post, he says, "FGM is not simply a women's issue, but a human rights issue. Men must start seeing it as such," and that "FGM has absolutely no place in Islam. There's nothing in the Quran about it."
<v Mariya Karimjee>在联合国发言后，他在Facebook上发了贴。那是一篇长篇且充满激情的文章，敦促波拉男性不要保持沉默并纵容FGM。在文中，他说：“FGM不仅是女性问题，更是人权问题。男性必须开始这样看待它。”他还说：“FGM在伊斯兰教中绝无容身之地。古兰经里对此只字未提。”

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:59.110
<v Mariya Karimjee>The video of my brother's speech made the rounds in the Bohri community. On Facebook, there was only a show of support. Bohris from around the world Liked my brother's stance on FGM.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我弟弟演讲的视频在波拉社区传开了。在Facebook上，只有支持的声音。来自世界各地的波拉人点赞了我弟弟反对FGM的立场。

00:32:59.110 --> 00:33:28.790
<v Mariya Karimjee>But less publicly, my brother had sparked some controversy. His private message folder filled up with messages from Bohris who disagreed. My parents received texts from Bohri friends in Pakistan, Tanzania, the UK, and the US, warning them that they may get in trouble with the clergy for this. A high-ranking clergy member's wife strongly discouraged me from attending further services at my mosque. Like a lot of Bohris in Karachi, Sameena saw my brother's post and showed it to her parents.
<v Mariya Karimjee>但私下里，我弟弟引发了一些争议。他的私人留言箱里充满了不同意这一立场的波拉人发来的信息。我父母收到了来自巴基斯坦、坦桑尼亚、英国和美国的波拉朋友发来的短信，警告他们可能会因为这件事在教职人员那里惹上麻烦。一位高级教职人员的妻子强烈劝阻我不要继续参加清真寺的礼拜。像卡拉奇的许多波拉人一样，萨米娜看到了我弟弟的贴子，并把它给父母看了。

00:33:28.790 --> 00:33:36.360
<v Sameena>And with this, when I told my mom that I saw this on Facebook, my mom specifically told me not to Like it. She said, don't Like it. Don't share it.
<v Sameena>就是这样，当我告诉我妈我在Facebook上看到了这个，我妈特地告诉我不许点赞。她说，别点赞。别分享。

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:56.260
<v Sameena>We were at home. My dad was there as well. This is what my dad said, that your sexual desire, you have to curb it just so that the girls don't go around. And so I said, but guys do it. My mom was like, why don't you question [GUJARATI] then? Why don't you question [GUJARATI]? Why are you questioning this? I was like, because it's being done to me!
<v Sameena>我们当时在家。我爸也在场。这就是我爸说的，你的性欲，你必须克制它，这样女孩就不会乱来。所以我说，但男人们也乱来啊。我妈就说，那你为什么不去质疑伊斯兰的教义呢？为什么要质疑这个？我说：因为这是发生在我身上的！

00:33:56.260 --> 00:34:14.540
<v Mariya Karimjee>Quick translation. Sameena's mom basically argued, why not question everything we believe then? Praying five times a day. Fasting. Every major tenet of Islam. As Sameena pointed out, none of those things are an assault on her body. Anyhow, Sameena's fight with her parents went on.
<v Mariya Karimjee>简短翻译一下。萨米娜的妈妈基本上反驳说：那为什么不质疑我们相信的一切呢？每天祈祷五次。禁食。伊斯兰教的每一项主要信条。正如萨米娜指出的，这些事情都没有对她的身体造成侵害。总之，萨米娜和父母的争吵还在继续。

00:34:14.540 --> 00:34:41.190
<v Sameena>And then I was like, if having sex is a bad thing, and if you don't want me to do it, teach me not to do it like you taught me not to drink and eat pork. I don't do it, right? Similarly, if you will have taught me not to sleep around, I probably wouldn't. I mean, if consciously I have controlled myself, that's the point in Islam, to have that willpower to not do something. I would never eat pork because I've been told all my life, you're not supposed to eat it. I'm never going to eat it.
<v Sameena>然后我说，如果性行为是一件坏事，如果你们不希望我做，那就教导我别做，就像教我别喝酒吃猪肉一样。我不做那事，对吧？同样，如果你们教我不乱搞，我大概也就不会了。我是说，如果我自觉地克制自己，那才是伊斯兰教的意义所在，拥有不去做某事的意志力。我永远不会吃猪肉，因为我从小被教育我不该吃。我永远不会吃。

00:34:41.190 --> 00:34:58.680
<v Sameena>The point is, I know this is something that I'm not supposed to do. If you told us what we were doing to ourselves, and then had done it with consent, then it's a different thing. But at an age where I don't know what it is, you do something to me which is irreversible. And you don't have to cut something off just to curb that desire.
<v Sameena>重点是，我知道这是我不该做的事。如果你们告诉我们我们正对自己做什么，然后我们达成共识，那是另一回事。但在一个我根本不知道这是什么的年纪，你们在我身上做了不可逆转的事情。而且为了克制那种欲望，并不非得切掉什么东西。

00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:03.100
<v Mariya Karimjee>Well, what did they say back to that specific argument?
<v Mariya Karimjee>嗯，他们对那个具体的观点有什么回应？

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.080
<v Sameena>Um--
<v Sameena>嗯——

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:13.720
<v Mariya Karimjee>Because I feel like it's a really good argument. You're like, OK, you've taught me right from wrong. So why is sex something that you didn't teach me? You just made a decision for me.
<v Mariya Karimjee>因为我觉得这是一个非常好的观点。你说，好吧，你们教了我什么是对，什么是错。所以为什么性行为是你们没教我的东西？你们只是替我做了一个决定。

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:23.500
<v Sameena>I knew they did not have a response because, well, what do you say to your child who is asking you such a question? Because I'm not even sure if they knew why exactly they were doing it.
<v Sameena>我知道他们没有回应，因为，你能对提出这种问题的孩子说什么呢？因为我甚至不确定他们是否知道自己为什么要这么做。

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:25.410
<v Mariya Karimjee>Did you feel like you'd won that argument?
<v Mariya Karimjee>你觉得你赢了那场争论吗？

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:26.960
<v Sameena>I think so, yes.
<v Sameena>我想是的，没错。

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:29.280
<v Mariya Karimjee>Do you think they thought that you'd won that argument?
<v Mariya Karimjee>你觉得他们认为你赢了那场争论吗？

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:34.730
<v Sameena>I think so. They didn't have an answer. Then my mom was like, well, I also pierced your ears, but you didn't say anything about that.
<v Sameena>我想是的。他们答不上来。然后我妈就说：嗯，我也给你打了耳洞，但你没说什么。

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:46.230
<v Mariya Karimjee>My mom said the same thing. She literally said the same thing. I was like, how is piercing my ears at six months old anything like getting my clitoris cut? Are you crazy? I cannot believe you would say that.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我妈也是这么说的。她真的是原话照搬。我说：在我六个月大时打耳洞，怎么能和切除阴蒂相提并论？你们疯了吗？我无法相信你会这么说。

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:47.110
<v Sameena>I know.
<v Sameena>我知道。

00:35:47.110 --> 00:35:48.410
<v Mariya Karimjee>Did that make you mad?
<v Mariya Karimjee>那让你生气吗？

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:55.360
<v Sameena>I was super mad, yes.
<v Sameena>我超级生气，是的。

00:35:55.360 --> 00:36:10.700
<v Mariya Karimjee>The thing I really wanted to know, though, was much more personal. It was the question that had been haunting me from the moment I found out that I had been cut. My question centered on the very thing her parents told her was the goal of FGM, curbing women's desire.
<v Mariya Karimjee>但我真正想知道的是，要个人化得多。那是从我发现自己被切割那一刻起就一直困扰我的问题。我的问题集中在她的父母告诉她FGM的目标上，即克制女性的欲望。

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:17.470
<v Mariya Karimjee>I definitely feel desire. I'm just kind of wondering, is that true of you? Do you get turned on?
<v Mariya Karimjee>我确实感觉到欲望。我只是有点好奇，你也是这样吗？你会感到兴奋吗？

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:20.080
<v Sameena>I do.
<v Sameena>是的。

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:21.290
<v Mariya Karimjee>You do get turned on?
<v Mariya Karimjee>你真的会感到兴奋？

00:36:21.290 --> 00:36:47.840
<v Sameena>I do. Usually-- I mean, mostly if I'm watching something sexy. Then yeah, I do. So every time I watch TV or something and I get turned on, and then I think about what my parents said, that you don't feel any sexual desire, here I'm thinking that they said that when you get cut, you don't feel any desire. But I am feeling it, so maybe this kind of circumcision, it's not affecting me.
<v Sameena>会。通常——我是说，如果我看到什么性感的东西。那时是的，我会。所以每次我看电视之类的东西并感到兴奋时，我就想到父母说的话，说你不会有任何性欲。我当时的理解是，他们说当你被切割后，你就不会有任何欲望。但我现在正感觉着，所以也许这种割礼，并没有影响到我。

00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:53.640
<v Mariya Karimjee>Sameena's not married, and she hasn't had sex. She's also not in pain the same way I am.
<v Mariya Karimjee>萨米娜还没结婚，也没有发生过性行为。她也没有像我这样痛苦。

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.260
<v Mariya Karimjee>Have you ever tried to masturbate?
<v Mariya Karimjee>你试过自慰吗？

00:36:56.260 --> 00:36:57.240
<v Sameena>I have.
<v Sameena>试过。

00:36:57.240 --> 00:36:59.110
<v Mariya Karimjee>And how'd that go for you?
<v Mariya Karimjee>感觉怎么样？

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:00.540
<v Sameena>It works.
<v Sameena>挺好的。

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:05.030
<v Mariya Karimjee>Well hey, that's so nice to hear. There is hope out there.
<v Mariya Karimjee>嘿，听到这个真好。外面还是有希望的。

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:22.000
<v Sameena>Yeah. The thing is, I've never spoken to anyone in detail who has had sex about how it feels and stuff, so I don't know. And I haven't spoken to anyone who has been cut and has had sex. That is the thing.
<v Sameena>是啊。问题是，我从未和任何有过性行为的人详细聊过感觉如何之类的话题，所以我不知道。而且我还没和任何同样被切割且发生过性行为的人聊过。这就是问题所在。

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:46.670
<v Sameena>Knowing that I've been cut, I can still masturbate. And I keep thinking that OK, maybe this is like a lesser level of orgasm or whatever feeling that I get. Maybe I don't feel as much desire as people who were not cut. Maybe if they hadn't done it, I would feel more desire than what I feel right now, maybe.
<v Sameena>知道自己被切割后，我依然可以自慰。但我一直在想，好吧，也许这只是较低水平的性高潮，或者无论我得到的是什么感觉。也许我感受到的欲望没有没被切割的人多。也许如果他们没那样做，我会比现在感受到更多欲望，也许吧。

00:37:46.670 --> 00:38:04.340
<v Mariya Karimjee>I told Sameena that I thought the whole idea of curbing desire is a myth and BS, and that saying that cutting limits a woman's desire is just a way to hide what the goal really is-- to take away our enjoyment of sex. Sameena didn't entirely agree.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我告诉萨米娜，我认为克制欲望的整体理念是一个神话，是胡扯。而且声称切割限制女性欲望只是为了掩盖真正的目标——剥夺我们享受性的权利。萨米娜并不完全同意。

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:19.610
<v Sameena>But I don't think that taking something away from us is their intention, because they're doing it without reason. I think for most parents-- like I know my friends' parents as well. I see my mom in each one of them. Their mothers and stuff, they're all like [GUJARATI].
<v Sameena>但我认为夺走我们的东西并不是他们的本意，因为他们做这件事时没理由。我想对大多数父母来说——就像我也认识我朋友的父母一样。我在她们每个人身上都看到了我妈的影子。她们的母亲什么的，她们都……

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:23.560
<v Mariya Karimjee>[GUJARATI]. It's a part of our culture, her mom said.
<v Mariya Karimjee>[古吉拉特语]

00:38:23.560 --> 00:39:06.900
<v Sameena>So they just do it without any reason, I guess. I mean, there's really not much logic. When you believe in something blindly, that's what they call faith. If everybody in the community is doing something, then it's something that you just do. I feel like it's like-- I don't know how to put this into words. My mother was the one who took me and did this to me. So I don't know if it's like a male-dominated thing, or if men don't want this for women, or if it's like just women have just taught themselves that they shouldn't enjoy this or something. I don't know if I'm making sense.
<v Sameena>[古吉拉特语]。这是我们文化的一部分，她妈妈说。

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:08.150
<v Mariya Karimjee>You're making perfect sense.
<v Mariya Karimjee>所以我想她们就是没理由地做了，我想。我是说，确实没什么逻辑。当你盲目地相信某样东西时，那就是所谓的信仰。如果社区里的每个人都在做某事，那你就跟着做。我觉得这就像——我不知道怎么用言语表达。是我妈妈带我去并对我做了这件事。所以我不知道这是否是一件男性主导的事情，或者男性不希望女性享受这个，或者仅仅是女性教会了自己不该享受这些东西。我不知道我表达得对不对。

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:09.010
<v Sameena>OK.
<v Sameena>你表达得非常清楚。

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:26.290
<v Mariya Karimjee>She was making perfect sense. I had wondered the same thing. Why were women still subjecting their daughters to this? Until recently, my grandmother didn't even know what the term FGM meant. Out of the blue, she asked me to explain it. She read about it in the paper.
<v Mariya Karimjee>好。

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:58.390
<v Mariya Karimjee>This time I told her about the bug story. I asked her if she remembered when I was cut and told her how wrong I thought the practice was. I asked her if she thought it was wrong. She mumbled something about the religious clergy wanting it done, and that she supposed they knew best. I was stunned. She seemed to have no idea what the surgery did exactly, what it took away. Before I left, my grandmother held my hand and said, "your mother tried to stop it."
<v Mariya Karimjee>她表达得非常清楚。我也一直在想同样的问题。为什么女性还在让她们的女儿经历这种事？直到最近，我祖母甚至不知道FGM这个词的意思。突如其来地，她让我解释一下。她在报纸上读到过。

00:39:58.390 --> 00:40:17.020
<v Mariya Karimjee>I didn't understand. I couldn't even process what she'd said. It took me weeks to speak to my mother about that conversation I'd had with my grandmother. When I finally did, it was so hard to bring up, I just whispered to my mom, "did you try and stop them from cutting me?"
<v Mariya Karimjee>这次我给她讲了虫子的故事。我问她是否记得我被切割的时候，并告诉她我觉得这种做法有多错误。我问她是否认为这是错的。她含糊其辞，说宗教教职人员想要这样做，她猜他们最清楚。我惊呆了。她似乎根本不知道手术到底做了什么，夺走了什么。离开前，我祖母握着我的手说：“你妈妈尝试过阻止。”

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:44.550
<v Mariya Karimjee>She explained it was my grandfather who had insisted that the women in the family be cut. He believed that if the religious clergy thought this was necessary, we were wrong to question it. The fights about having me cut grew so out of control that he threatened to kick my mother out of the family. My grandmother was silent about it all. After my mother told me what really happened, our relationship changed. My anger towards her was gone almost immediately.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我不明白。我甚至无法处理她说的话。花了几个星期，我才和我妈谈起我和祖母的那次谈话。最终谈及时，这太难开口了，我只是低声对妈妈说：“你当时试着阻止他们切割我了吗？”

00:40:44.550 --> 00:41:08.430
<v Mariya Karimjee>I wish I could tell you definitively that things are changing among Bohris. Mainly because of the secrecy, people don't really say where they stand. After my brother spoke at the UN, my father was proud of him. He apologized to me for what happened. He said it's like if someone cut off his penis. I said, yep. He shared my brother's Facebook post with more than 50 of his friends.
<v Mariya Karimjee>她解释说，是我祖父坚持要家里女性必须被切割。他相信如果宗教教职人员认为这有必要，我们质疑就是错的。关于是否给我切割的争吵到了失控的地步，他威胁要把我妈逐出家门。祖母对此保持沉默。在我妈告诉我真相后，我们的关系改变了。我对她的愤怒几乎立刻消失了。

00:41:08.430 --> 00:41:28.620
<v Mariya Karimjee>And then within days, he lost his confidence. He got word from a close family member that he was on the wrong side of this and he shouldn't criticize the practice-- publicly. And "publicly" is the thing. He stopped sharing the post.
<v Mariya Karimjee>我真希望能确切地告诉你波拉人中间的事情正在改变。主要是因为秘密，人们并没真正说出他们的立场。我弟弟在联合国发言后，我父亲为他感到自豪。他为发生在我身上的事向我道歉。他说这就像有人割了他的阴茎一样。我说，是的。他把他弟弟的Facebook贴子分享给了50多位朋友。

00:41:28.620 --> 00:42:02.140
<v Mariya Karimjee>Recently a Bohri leader in Sydney was prosecuted and sent to jail for FGM. After that, a handful of clergy from the Bohri community around the world started circulating letters saying that cutting is illegal and we should stop doing it. That's on the heels of a small group of us who have become very vocal about ending FGM. That's what I can do. That's a new Bohri tradition.
<v Mariya Karimjee>几天之内，他失去了信心。他从一位亲密的家庭成员那里获悉，他在这件事上站在了错误的一边，不应该公开批评这种习俗。而“公开”才是关键。他停止分享那个贴子了。

