<v Sean Cole>我记得有一次谈话。他当时说，我干脆把它们扔了吧。很生气地说，我要把它们扔掉。

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:43.770
<v Neda Semnani>I was like, "don't!" "Do you know what's in them?" He's like, "no." I was like, "well then, you [BLEEP]ing can't throw them away."
<v Neda Semnani>这是内达，内马的姐姐。她大三岁。她对这些箱子的感觉非常不同。

00:44:43.770 --> 00:45:04.770
<v Sean Cole>So Nema tucks away the boxes and the filing cabinet in a storage unit outside Washington, DC. And then a few years after their mom dies, Neda's trying to piece together the details of their parents' lives and what happened to their father. And she says to Nema, can we go check out that storage unit? So they head over there, and it's like a Hardy Boys episode.
<v Sean Cole>我说，“别！”“你知道里面有什么吗？”他说，“不知道。”我说，“那你就该死的不能把它们扔掉。”

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:38.960
<v Neda Semnani>For some reason we're the only two there, but I feel like in storage units, it always feels like you're the only one there. And we opened the door of the storage unit. And there's all this crap. There's clothes and there's whatever, and there's a rusted-out filing cabinet. And I open it. And literally all by itself in this mass of stuff is just this, just this folder with my dad's name on it, sitting very patiently, as if it had been waiting for me all this time.
<v Neda Semnani>于是内马把箱子和文件柜塞进了华盛顿特区外的一个储藏室。母亲去世几年后，内达试图拼凑父母的生活细节以及父亲遭遇的事情。她对内马说：我们可以去看看那个储藏室吗？于是他们去了那儿，感觉就像《哈代兄弟》的一集。

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:47.540
<v Sean Cole>It was a trove of information about their father. And it wasn't just that one folder. In another drawer there were more files, and more in some of the boxes.
<v Sean Cole>因为某种原因，只有我们两个人去，但我感觉在储藏室里，总是感觉只有你一个人。我们打开了储藏室的门。全是破烂。有衣服什么的，还有一个生锈的文件柜。我打开它。在这堆东西里，有一个文件夹孤零零地立在那儿，上面写着我爸爸的名字，非常耐心地坐着，仿佛一直在等我。

00:45:47.540 --> 00:45:57.690
<v Neda Semnani>And a few days later, I'm going through them, or maybe it was the next day. There are handwritten letters and Dad's passport.
<v Neda Semnani>那是一个关于他们父亲的信息宝库。不仅有那一个文件夹。在另一个抽屉里还有更多文件，箱子里也有更多。

00:45:57.690 --> 00:45:59.160
<v Sean Cole>She showed it to me.
<v Sean Cole>几天后，我正在翻看它们，或者也许是第二天。里面有手写信件和爸爸的护照。

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:00.400
<v Sean Cole>Can I look at his picture?
<v Sean Cole>她给我看了。

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:02.290
<v Neda Semnani>Oh, yes.
<v Neda Semnani>我可以看看他的照片吗？

00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:03.490
<v Sean Cole>Handsome guy.
<v Sean Cole>哦，是的。

00:46:03.490 --> 00:46:18.000
<v Neda Semnani>Yeah, he looks a lot like Nema, if Nema shaved. And I just remember thinking. I felt like-- I don't know. It felt like I was on the right path.
<v Neda Semnani>帅哥。

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:50.680
<v Sean Cole>This is what was in the box of bad, what Neda began finding out more and more about as she dug through the folders and files. So their parents were both from Iran. They met when they were students at Berkeley in California in the '60s. And they became involved in a leftist political movement there that opposed the shah of Iran. There was the revolution in '79. The shah goes into exile. And all of these leftists who had been agitating from abroad come streaming back into the country, back into Iran, high on the possibility of new freedoms in their country.
<v Sean Cole>是的，如果内马刮了胡子，他长得很像内马。我只记得当时的想法。我觉得——我不知道。感觉就像我走在了正确的道路上。

00:46:50.680 --> 00:47:15.500
<v Sean Cole>Neda and Nema's parents were two of them. Except the shah was replaced by the Ayatollah Khomeini, an Islamic fundamentalist who, arguably, was even more restrictive. And when the leftists-- the secular leftists-- agitated against this new regime, a lot of them were imprisoned, including Neda and Nema's dad. Their mother was still pregnant with Nema at the time. Neda was two years old.
<v Sean Cole>这就是“坏东西箱子”里的东西，也就是内达在翻看文件夹和文件时开始发现的越来越多的秘密。他们的父母都来自伊朗。他们在60年代加州伯克利做学生时相识。他们参与了一个反抗伊朗国王的左翼政治运动。1979年发生了革命。国王流亡。而这些一直在海外鼓动的左翼人士成群结队地回到伊朗，沉醉在国家出现新自由的可能性中。

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:31.220
<v Sean Cole>She and her mother escaped back to the States. Nema was born about a month after they got there. And then, four months after that, their dad and 20 other activists were executed. And as Neda got older, this image of her father formed in her head.
<v Sean Cole>内达和内马的父母就在其中。但国王被大阿亚图拉霍梅尼取代了，那是一位伊斯兰原教旨主义者，可以说比国王更为严苛。当左翼人士——世俗左翼——反对这个新政权时，他们中的许多人被监禁，包括内达和内马的爸爸。妈妈当时还怀着内马。内达才两岁。

00:47:31.220 --> 00:47:43.430
<v Neda Semnani>I believed he was a superhero. He was kind of a weird superhero, though, because no one really talked about him very much. So I think maybe that's part of the reason I thought, maybe he's still in prison.
<v Neda Semnani>她和母亲逃回了美国。内马在他们到达一个月后出生。然后，四个月后，他们的父亲和其他20名活动家被处决。随着内达长大，父亲的形象在她脑海中形成了。

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:53.530
<v Neda Semnani>I remember asking Mom, how do you know he's dead? We left before he died, so how do you know? Couldn't he have been like a ninja?
<v Neda Semnani>我相信他是个超级英雄。不过他是个古怪的超级英雄，因为没人真谈论他。所以我认为这可能是我认为他还在监狱里的原因。

00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:54.300
<v Sean Cole>You said ninja.
<v Sean Cole>我记得问妈妈，你是怎么知道他死了的？我们在他死前就离开了，你怎么知道？他不能像忍者一样吗？

00:47:54.300 --> 00:48:07.000
<v Neda Semnani>Yeah. I don't know why ninja. Or like karate. Maybe I said karate. Couldn't he have done karate and gotten out?
<v Neda Semnani>你说忍者。

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:37.360
<v Sean Cole>30 years later, every new fact that Neda uncovers about her dad is like a strand of DNA she can use to re-engineer him, to bring him back. For example, Neda learned that in Tehran, after someone informed on their father, he and Neda's mother and Neda all went into hiding. Neda only has shadowy memories of this-- more like memories of memories. She certainly didn't know back then that her family was essentially waiting for the government to come and arrest everyone. And they figured they wouldn't be waiting long.
<v Sean Cole>是啊。我不知道为什么要说忍者。或者像是空手道。也许我说的是空手道。难道他不能学会空手道然后逃出来吗？

00:48:37.360 --> 00:49:02.070
<v Neda Semnani>But then a week went by, two weeks went by, a month went by. And then January was February, February was March, and still nothing. And apparently the stress of it had gotten to my dad. One side of his face kind of froze and stopped working for a month or something, just like the left side of his-- or one side of his face just fell.
<v Neda Semnani>30年后，内达关于父亲发现的每一个新事实，都像是一条她可以用来重新构建他的DNA，让他起死回生。例如，内达得知在德黑兰，在有人举报父亲后，他和内达的母亲以及内达都躲了起来。内达对那段经历只有模糊的记忆——更像是记忆的记忆。她那时肯定不知道家人本质上是在等待政府来抓捕所有人。他们认为自己等不了多久。

00:49:02.070 --> 00:49:27.410
<v Sean Cole>Every time she found out a detail like this, she'd want to tell her brother, to share it with him. This is what Mom and Dad went through, our mom and dad. This is where we come from. But Nema, her brother, wasn't interested. Not that he wasn't interested, he aggressively did not want to know any of these details she was finding out. This whole project of exhuming the past, re-animating their parents, what did he have to gain from that?
<v Sean Cole>但随后一周过去了，两周过去了，一个月过去了。一月变成了二月，二月变成了三月，依然什么都没发生。显然压力折磨着父亲。他半边脸冻住不动了，好几个月还是什么，只是一边脸瘫痪了。

00:49:27.410 --> 00:49:37.680
<v Nema Semnani>She has certain fragments and puzzle pieces of the father. I don't have any of that. So I got the privilege of creating him.
<v Nema Semnani>每次她发现像这样的细节，她就想告诉弟弟，和他分享。这是父母经历过的事，我们的爸妈。我们来自这里。但内马，她弟弟，不感兴趣。不是说他不感兴趣，而是他积极地不想知道她发现的任何这些细节。挖掘过去、复活父母的整个项目，对他有什么好处？

00:49:37.680 --> 00:49:40.730
<v Sean Cole>It's funny that you would think of that as a privilege.
<v Sean Cole>她有关于父亲的一些碎片和拼图块。我什么都没有。所以我才有幸去创造他。

00:49:40.730 --> 00:49:49.200
<v Nema Semnani>I can't-- how else would you think of it? Obviously it sucks. I'm not going to say it doesn't suck. But right now my father is perfect.
<v Nema Semnani>你竟然认为这是特权，真有意思。

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:56.360
<v Nema Semnani>I already have 100 out of 100 right now. It can only go down. There's a very good chance that you know more than I do.
<v Nema Semnani>我不能——不然还能怎么想？显而易见，这很糟糕。我不打算说它不糟糕。但现在，我的父亲是完美的。

00:49:56.360 --> 00:50:00.020
<v Sean Cole>No, no, no, no. Nema, I certainly know more than you do.
<v Sean Cole>我已经得了100分，满分。它只能降不能升。很有可能你知道的比我多。

00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:00.880
<v Nema Semnani>OK, good.
<v Nema Semnani>不，不，不，不。内马，我肯定比你知道的多。

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:07.810
<v Sean Cole>Which is a funny feeling because it's your history. I mean, it's your family.
<v Sean Cole>好吧，那就好。

00:50:07.810 --> 00:50:09.370
<v Nema Semnani>Yeah.
<v Nema Semnani>这是一种奇怪的感觉，因为这是你的历史。我是说，这是你的家庭。

00:50:09.370 --> 00:50:32.370
<v Sean Cole>Whereas Neda is swimming in the details of the revolution and the escape and the execution practically every day-- she's writing a book about it, actually-- and those details are sometimes hard to carry around. A bit of what she's learned. And here I'll just issue a personal content advisory warning to Nema, who, if you're listening to this and you still don't want to know this stuff, you should turn down the radio for a minute.
<v Sean Cole>是啊。

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:53.600
<v Sean Cole>Firstly, the group their dad was part of staged an armed uprising against Khomeini. It was a terrible plan, and it went badly. The irony is that their dad opposed the uprising, didn't take part in it. But it didn't matter. The governmental guards arrested him, bound his hands behind him with a rope, and marched him back to his parents' house.
<v Sean Cole>而内达每天几乎都沉浸在革命、逃亡和处决的细节中——实际上她正在写一本书——而这些细节有时很难承载。她发现的一些东西。在这里，我给内马发一个个人内容警告：如果你正在听这个，而且仍然不想知道这些东西，你应该把收音机关掉一分钟。

00:50:53.600 --> 00:51:22.580
<v Sean Cole>This whole eerie scene ensues there, with their dad, their aunt, and these four armed guards sitting around, waiting for their mother to arrive. She doesn't arrive. She calls instead. The aunt says, everything's OK. Bring Neda to your mother's place. It was code. Her mother lived in California.
<v Sean Cole>首先，父亲所属的组织策划了一场针对霍梅尼的武装起义。那是个糟糕的计划，进行得很糟。讽刺的是，父亲反对那场起义，并没有参加。但这并不重要。政府卫兵逮捕了他，用绳子把他的手反绑在身后，把他押回了他父母的房子里。

00:51:22.580 --> 00:51:49.190
<v Sean Cole>The escape through Iranian Kurdistan was harrowing and took days. They rode horses and walked and were driven by smugglers-- smugglers who abandoned them on the roadside twice. They were desperately thirsty and hungry. Neda went half crazy for a little bit from heatstroke. Again, she was two. Their mom, pregnant with Nema, fell down an embankment at one point but climbed back up and kept walking.
<v Sean Cole>整个诡异的场景就在那里发生了，他和阿姨，以及四个武装警卫坐在那里，等着母亲到来。她没来，而是打来了电话。阿姨说：一切都好。带内达去你妈妈那儿。那是暗号。她母亲住在加州。

00:51:49.190 --> 00:52:03.660
<v Sean Cole>Meantime, Neda and Nema's dad was being held in solitary confinement at Evin prison. Neda describes his cell block as a prison within a prison, filled with torture devices. So Neda understands Nema not wanting to know these things.
<v Sean Cole>穿越伊朗库尔德斯坦的逃亡极其艰辛，花了几天时间。他们骑马步行，由走私者带路——走私者两次把他们遗弃在路边。他们极度饥渴。内达一度因为中暑半疯了。再一次，她才两岁。母亲怀着内马，有一次跌下路基，但爬回来继续走。

00:52:03.660 --> 00:52:23.440
<v Nema Semnani>That's another thing, is Neda knows about the torture. He was tortured in these things, and she knows about it. It's the difference between knowing that it happened and maybe even knowing the methods at which they were implemented. And I'm pretty sure she knows these things. Put that very high on the list of things I don't want to know. And why the hell does she want to know? Right?
<v Nema Semnani>同时，内达和内马的父亲被单独囚禁在埃文监狱。内达形容他的牢房是一个监狱里的监狱，装满了酷刑工具。所以内达理解内马为什么不想知道这些事。

00:52:23.440 --> 00:52:35.780
<v Sean Cole>Nema learns about these things almost by accident. Through his earmuffs, as he says, things just creep in now and then. And yeah, the torture is very high on that list of things he doesn't want to know. But practically everything's on that list.
<v Sean Cole>这也是一回事，内达知道酷刑的事。他在那些东西里受到了拷打，而她知道相关细节。这就像知道事情发生了和可能知道实施的具体手段之间的区别。我肯定她知道这些东西。把那放在我不想要知道的清单的最顶端。到底为什么她想知道？对吧？

00:52:35.780 --> 00:52:58.430
<v Neda Semnani>He's allowed to decide the stories he tells himself, I think. I mean, they're his parents too. I mean, I think that there's stuff that he should know, or at least be given the option to. So when someone had sent us the footage of Dad's trial, I sent it to him. And then he told me a couple days ago that he watched, like, a second of it.
<v Neda Semnani>内马几乎是偶然了解到这些事情的。正如他所说，戴着耳罩，东西偶尔会溜进来。是的，拷打在不想知道的清单上名列前茅。事实上，几乎所有东西都在那个清单上。

00:52:58.430 --> 00:53:07.380
<v Nema Semnani>It was just long enough to see-- I watched it for 30 seconds, caring not what he said but how he said it.
<v Nema Semnani>我认为他有权决定告诉自己什么样的故事。我是说，他们也是他的父母。我想有些事他应该知道，或者至少有选择知道的权利。所以当有人寄给我们父亲受审的视频时，我发给了他。然后他两天前告诉我，他看了大概一秒钟。

00:53:07.380 --> 00:53:08.600
<v Sean Cole>You just wanted to--
<v Sean Cole>时间足够久到看到——我看了30秒，不在乎他说什么，而是怎么说。

00:53:08.600 --> 00:53:16.810
<v Nema Semnani>I wanted to see. Does he do what I do? Does he tilt his head a little bit? Does he speak with his hands? Does he gesticulate a lot?
<v Nema Semnani>你只是想——

00:53:16.810 --> 00:53:20.730
<v Sean Cole>And did he do any of that stuff?
<v Sean Cole>我想看看。他有我那样的习惯吗？他会稍微歪着头吗？他会用手势说话吗？他有很多肢体动作吗？

00:53:20.730 --> 00:53:23.250
<v Nema Semnani>Not that I could really tell.
<v Nema Semnani>他有做那些动作吗？

00:53:23.250 --> 00:54:03.860
<v Sean Cole>Their dad doesn't move around a lot in the video, actually. His hands are resting on a table. He looks tired, defeated. In certain moments, he seems near tears. It's not the kind of video you watch when you want to have a sunny idea of where you come from. Still, in a way, Nema had the same experience watching that little bit of the video that Neda has had this whole time in all of her research. He says it proved that his dad was a living person, that he wasn't a myth. But he doesn't think he's going to watch any more of it.
<v Sean Cole>我没太看出来。

00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:24.020
<v Sean Cole>It's funny, but Neda and Nema's mom knew they'd end up each feeling the way they do about the past. She all but said it in an interview with StoryCorps, that traveling recording booth where people tell stories about their lives. In this case, their mom was just talking to one of the StoryCorps volunteers, who asked her what her late husband would have thought of the family's life in America.
<v Sean Cole>其实，父亲在视频里没怎么动。他的手放在桌上。他看起来疲惫、挫败。在某些时刻，他似乎快要哭出来了。这不是你想拥有阳光背景时会看的视频。尽管如此，从某种程度上，内马在观看那一点点视频时，体验和内达在所有研究中一直经历的是一样的。他说这证明了父亲是一个活生生的人，他不是神话。但他觉得他不会再看了。

00:54:24.020 --> 00:55:11.310
<v Mrs. Semnani>And more than anything, I think he would have been proud of his two children. He never saw his son. And his daughter, who was the apple of his eyes, they didn't see each other after she was two and a half. So his sort of ghost hovers over my daughter. But my son, who never knew him, I think has a longing for a father he never knew. But there is not a ghost hovering over him, as there is one for my daughter.
<v Mrs. Semnani>这很有意思，内达和内马的妈妈知道他们最终会以自己的方式面对过去。她在采访StoryCorps时几乎说了出来，那是人们讲述生活故事的旅行录音棚。在这种情况下，妈妈只是和一个StoryCorps志愿者聊天，志愿者问她丈夫如果看到家人们在美国的生活会怎么想。

00:55:11.310 --> 00:55:45.980
<v Sean Cole>Neda first heard that recording long after their mother died. Nema has never heard it, unless he's listening to this right now. But I did tell him what she said. And he said, that's 100% accurate. She was a smart woman.
<v Sean Cole>我想最重要的是，他会为他的两个孩子感到自豪。他没见过儿子。而女儿，曾是他掌上明珠的女儿，她在两岁半之后就没见过他。所以他的鬼魂某种程度上盘旋在女儿身上。但我儿子，从未认识他，我认为他渴望一个从未谋面的父亲。但盘旋在他身上的不是鬼魂，就像女儿身上有一个。

00:55:45.980 --> 00:55:57.190
<v Sean Cole>[MUSIC - "WHAT I AM" BY EDIE BRICKELL & NEW BOHEMIANS]
<v Sean Cole>内达在母亲去世很久后才听到那段录音。内马从未听过，除非他现在正在收听。但我告诉了他母亲说的话。他说，那完全准确。她是个聪明的女人。

00:55:57.190 --> 00:56:20.550
<v Sean Cole>Our program was produced today by Neil Drumming and Nancy Updike. Our production staff includes Zoe Chace, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, Lyra Smith, and Matt Tierney. Our editor is Joel Lovell. Editorial help from Julie Snyder and Elna Baker. Other staff, Elise Bergerson, Emily Condon, Kimberly Henderson, and Seth Lind. Research help from Christopher Swetala. Music help from Damien Graef and Rob Geddis.
